1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,375
The Search for Dark Matter.

2
00:00:02,325 --> 00:00:11,600
Dark matter constitutes approximately 27% of the universe's mass-energy content, while ordinary baryonic matter accounts for only 5%.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:17,875
The remaining 68% is dark energy, a separate phenomenon driving cosmic acceleration.

4
00:00:17,875 --> 00:00:26,050
Dark matter neither emits, absorbs, nor reflects electromagnetic radiation, making it completely invisible to telescopes.

5
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:30,500
Its existence was first inferred from galactic rotation curves.

6
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:41,800
Astronomer Vera Rubin's observations in the 1970s showed that stars at the outer edges of spiral galaxies orbit at velocities far too high for visible matter alone to bind them gravitationally.

7
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:48,050
This discrepancy requires an invisible halo of mass roughly five times greater than the visible mass.

8
00:00:48,050 --> 00:01:04,037
Additional evidence comes from gravitational lensing, where dark matter's gravitational field bends light from background galaxies, and from the Bullet Cluster, where gravitational lensing maps reveal mass displaced from visible gas — direct observational evidence of collisionless dark matter.

9
00:01:04,037 --> 00:01:11,200
The Planck satellite's measurements of the cosmic microwave background provide precise constraints on the total dark matter density.

10
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:17,450
The leading particle candidates are weakly interacting massive particles (WIMPs) and axions.

11
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:25,300
WIMPs arise naturally in supersymmetric extensions of the Standard Model and would interact only through gravity and the weak nuclear force.

12
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:39,250
The LUX-ZEPLIN (LZ) experiment, located 1.5 kilometers underground at the Sanford Underground Research Facility in South Dakota, uses 10 tonnes of liquid xenon to search for WIMP-nucleus scattering events.

13
00:01:39,250 --> 00:01:44,675
It set the most stringent limits on WIMP-nucleon cross-sections as of 2024.

14
00:01:44,675 --> 00:01:55,050
Axions, hypothetical particles proposed to solve the strong CP problem in quantum chromodynamics, are searched for using resonant cavity detectors such as ADMX.

15
00:01:55,050 --> 00:02:04,237
Despite decades of increasingly sensitive searches spanning 40 orders of magnitude in possible particle mass, no direct detection has been confirmed.

16
00:02:04,237 --> 00:02:11,537
A definitive detection would represent the first extension of the Standard Model of particle physics driven by astronomical evidence.

